# UI test: a write to a binding that was never declared `mutable` (SC0304).
#
# `syntax-revision-2.md` §2.2 writes `let mutable i be 0` before `i be i + 1`,
# and that word would mean nothing if the plain `let` also admitted the write.
# Until this code existed it did: `mutable` parsed, resolved, reached
# `hir::PatternKind::Binding` — and nothing ever read it, so this file checked,
# built, and printed `3`.
#
# Two spellings are here together because the fix differs and the note is the
# whole value of the diagnostic: a `let` takes the word, and a parameter has
# nowhere to put it.
#
# A pattern binding — `for mutable i in …` — is the third spelling and is not
# tested here, because the rule declines wherever the type did not come out
# and `for i in 0..3` has no type at all: a range is `Ty::ERROR` until `Range`
# exists in the prelude (see `SC0538`). That is a real gap, stated rather than
# papered over.

def bump(n: I64) -> I64:
    n be n + 1
    return n

def main() -> Error?:
    let count: I64 be 0
    count be 3
    print(f"{bump(count)}")
    null
